{
 "qid": "Q229",
 "names": [
  "Republic of Cyprus"
 ],
 "title": "Republic of Cyprus",
 "variantOf": "Q229",
 "endonym": {
  "native": "Κυπριακή Δημοκρατία",
  "roman": "Kypriaki Dimokratia"
 },
 "capsule": "The Republic of Cyprus is the modern sovereign state proclaimed on 16 August 1960 under the Zurich-London Agreements, ending British colonial rule and establishing a power-sharing constitution between the island's Greek Cypriot majority and Turkish Cypriot minority under President Makarios III. Communal breakdown in the 1960s and a Greek-backed coup in 1974 led to the Turkish invasion of July-August 1974, after which the Republic's effective jurisdiction was confined to the southern two-thirds of the island, with the north under a separate, internationally unrecognized Turkish Cypriot administration. Since 1 May 2004 the Republic has been a member of the European Union, though EU law is suspended in the north pending reunification, and UN-brokered talks, most recently at Crans-Montana in 2017, have repeatedly failed to produce a settlement.",
 "citations": [
  {
   "label": "History of Cyprus - Wikipedia",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Cyprus"
  },
  {
   "label": "Cyprus problem - Wikipedia",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyprus_problem"
  }
 ],
 "edited": "era-variant",
 "attestation": "Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The existence and general terms of the Cyprus condominium are attested in Byzantine and Arab chronicles, but the island's precise administrative division and the degree of day-to-day joint governance are debated among historians of the early medieval Mediterranean. Capsule researched for this polity/era specifically (confidence: high).",
 "attestationLinks": [
  {
